<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lumbini</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lumbini is a site in the Rupandehi district of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, near the Indian border, revered as the birthplace of Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> visited it and set up an inscribed pillar around 249 BCE recording the Buddha's birth there, and the Khasa Malla king Ripumalla added his own inscription in 1312. Rediscovered in 1896, it is a World Heritage Site and a major place of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lumbini.jpg" alt="Lumbini" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bibek Raj Pandeya · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BRP_Lumbini_Mayadevi_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lumbini, whose name means the lovely or beautiful garden, is a Buddhist pilgrimage site in the Rupandehi District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. According to sacred texts and Buddhist commentaries, Queen Maya gave birth there to Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, about 563 BCE. In the Buddha's time it lay east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a> and south-west of Devadaha, in the territory of Shakya, an oligarchic republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutta-nipata">Sutta Nipata</a> states that he was born in a village of the Sakyans in the Lumbineyya Janapada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was not known as Lumbini until the discovery of the Ashoka Pillar. In 1896 the former Nepalese Army general Khadga Shamsher Jang Bahadur Rana and Alois Anton Führer found a great stone pillar at Rupandehi, guided by the records of the Chinese monk-pilgrims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>. Its Brahmi inscription shows that Ashoka, emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, visited in the 3rd century BCE and identified the place as the Buddha's birthplace. He made the village of Lumbini free of taxes and subject to only an eighth share of the produce. A second inscription at the top of the pillar was made by King Ripumalla in the Saka year 1234.</p><p>Excavations at the Mayadevi Temple in 2013 revealed beneath a brick shrine of the Ashokan era an older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> structure that appears to be an ancient tree shrine. Robin Coningham argues that human activity began around 1000 BCE and that the site may have been a Buddhist monument from the 6th century BCE. Other scholars state that the excavations revealed nothing Buddhist and show only that the site predates the Buddha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lumbini is one of the four most sacred pilgrimage sites of the Buddha's life, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a>. The complex is 4.8 kilometres long and 1.6 wide and has a Sacred Garden with the Mayadevi Temple, the Ashoka Pillar and the Puskarini sacred pond. A monastic zone is divided into an eastern zone for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> monasteries and a western zone for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> ones. Lumbini was made a World Heritage Site by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lumbini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buddhism began with the life and teaching of Siddhartha Gautama, a member of the Shakya clan who was born, according to tradition, at <b>Lumbini</b> in what is now southern Nepal.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ripumalla and Prithvimalla were among the most powerful rulers; Ripumalla left an inscription on the Ashokan pillar at <b>Lumbini</b> in 1312.</blockquote><a href="/state/khasa-malla-kingdom">Khasa Malla kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
